  "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B032",
        "laureates": [
          "Jens Juul Holst",
          "Svetlana Mojsov",
          "Daniel J. Drucker"
        ],
        "citation": "for discoveries of GLP-1 physiology that enabled transformative treatments for diabetes and obesity"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B047"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B032 most convincingly joins a discernible physiological discovery to an exceptionally large, durable therapeutic transformation. Holst, Mojsov, and Drucker provide a coherent account spanning foundational GLP-1 physiology and identification through biological and translational understanding. The mechanism and major clinical benefits in diabetes and obesity have been established over decades.",
    "case_against": "The route from GLP-1 science to widely used medicines involved consequential drug-development work, and three names cannot fully represent that pathway. The principal difficulty is not maturity or impact, but setting a defensible boundary between foundational discovery and later therapeutic development.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson argues that Lotte Bjerre Knudsen offers a coherent three-person account of therapeutic development alongside Holst and Drucker, while recognizing Mojsov's foundational claim.",
        "response": "That qualification is important. I nevertheless retain Mojsov because peptide identification is closer to the stated GLP-1 discovery, while Drucker supplies the essential biological and translational bridge. Knudsen's therapeutic-development contribution remains a serious attributional alternative rather than a reason to dilute the discovery-centered citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius emphasizes that Feldmann and Maini offer an unusually clear, tightly linked discovery-to-clinic pairing for TNF blockade.",
        "response": "I agree that B003 has notably clean attribution, exceptional maturity, and should remain a leading alternative. B032 is preferable here because the GLP-1 achievement combines an equally well-established physiological foundation with a broader recent transformation of two major disease burdens, notwithstanding its more difficult allocation of credit."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "sandberg-rickard",
        "point": "Sandberg notes that the relative credit due to drug-development contributors, including Knudsen, is difficult to delimit within the three-laureate limit.",
        "response": "I agree fully with the attribution concern. The proposed citation should therefore remain anchored to discoveries of GLP-1 physiology, rather than purporting to award every component of pharmaceutical development."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the best three-person allocation should include a therapeutic-development representative rather than Mojsov remains unresolved.",
      "The appropriate wording must distinguish the GLP-1 discovery from the broader, distributed development of incretin medicines."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
